    "field": "Climate response theory",
    "problem": "Predicting climate response to various forcings is challenging, as direct simulations for every scenario are computationally prohibitive and existing theoretical methods, like the fluctuation-dissipation theorem, are prone to error when applied to complex, nonequilibrium systems like the climate.",
    "method": "This paper proposes a method using response theory to predict climate change by deriving a linear Green function from test perturbation model runs, which allows for predicting the climate system's response to arbitrary forcing scenarios through simple convolution.",
    "task": "The task is to apply response theory from nonequilibrium statistical mechanics to a general circulation model to predict changes in global averages and spatial patterns of surface temperature and precipitation resulting from increased CO2 concentration.",
    "model": [
        "PLASIM",
        "PUMA",
        "FAMOUS"
    ],
